  "query_preview": "..\nNumerous heavy showers and isolated thunderstorms moved across Guam \nthrough much of the day today. Some 2 to 4 inches of rain were \nreported by rain gauges and reports from observers. The afternoon \nrainfall total at WFO Guam was 2.35 inches. Additional showers and \nthunderstorms are expected tonight, which could lead to flooding \nconcerns, especially across southern Guam. Shower coverage looks to \ndecrease Friday through Sunday as the southern end of the monsoon \ndecreases in shower activity. As the pattern dries out, breezy \nconditions will return this weekend. \n\nUncertainty remains high in the evolution of the broader pattern \nnext week as several disturbances look to interact with the monsoon \ntrough. Model guidance is in a relatively good agreement that there \nwill be a few disturbances racing eastward north of the CNMI next \nweek, however models vary greatly on both location and intensity. \nSome guidance bring a couple of disturbances further south into the \nFar Northern Marianas Islands. This scenario shifts the monsoon \nsurge south over the CNMI and Guam, leading to higher impacts from \nwind and rain next week. Other guidance shows the disturbances \neither moving further north, or becoming more organized. This \nscenario shifts the strongest winds and rain north of CNMI and Guam. \nOverall, direct impacts from these disturbances are not expected for \nthe CNMI and Guam, but the influence on the monsoon surge will \ngreatly depend on where the disturbances track and how strong they \nbecome. If trends shift the monsoon surge southward next week, then \nhydrological products may be needed for the Marianas next week.",
